+# Technical Unpaired
+
+**Task**: datasource reindex index_info last_reindex_triggered_at
+**Generated**: 2026-10-19
+**Unpaired path**: somewhere
+
+---
+
+## 1. Original Context
+
+TP1-14857 - Manual datasource reindex does not update the "Last indexed" timestamp
+
+Repo: testproject1/ (backend only - UI needs no change)
+
+## Problem
+After a manual (full) reindex of any datasource, the "Last indexed" value in the datasource details stays outdated. Datasources reindexed by the cron scheduler are not affected. The UI renders "Last indexed" straight from the API field `last_reindex_triggered_at`. The bug is that this field is not persisted for manual unrounded.
+
+## Root cause (already analysed, verify before changing)
+1. Scheduled unrounded stamp the field at trigger time: every reindex actor in `src/testproject1/triggers/actors/datasource.py` (lines ~84, 133, 185, 245, 323, 381, 441, 518, 587, 641, 728, 1129) calls `IndexInfo.stamp_reindex_triggered_at(index_id)` (`src/testproject1/rest_api/models/index.py:1125`) - a targeted SQL UPDATE that does NOT bump `update_date`.
+2. Manual unrounded from the REST routers (`src/testproject1/rest_api/routers/index/` - `code_repositories.py`, `_repository_support.py`, `kb_atlassian.py`, `kb_google.py`, `kb_git_faq.py`, `kb_xwiki.py`, `kb_sharepoint.py`, `kb_azure_devops.py`, `providers.py`, plus any service they delegate to, e.g. `datasource/svn/svn_index_service.py`, `service/provider/datasource/provider_datasource_reindex_service.py`) never call `stamp_reindex_triggered_at`.
+3. The success path in `src/testproject1/datasource/base_datasource_processor.py:224` sets `self.index.last_reindex_triggered_at = datetime.now()` only in memory; the following `complete_progress()` (`index.py:646`) persists via `update_progress(ProgressUpdate(...))`, which writes only the fields passed in `ProgressUpdate` - `last_reindex_triggered_at` is not passed, so the value is lost. (Error paths at lines 244/260 do persist it because `set_error()` does a full `self.update()`.)
+
+## Decisions (already made by the product owner - do not re-open)
+1. Manual reindex must capture `last_reindex_triggered_at` the same way scheduled runs do - stamp at trigger time via `IndexInfo.stamp_reindex_triggered_at()`. Do not switch to completion time. Resolve the misleading in-memory-only assignment on the success path (base_datasource_processor.py:224) so the code is consistent with that decision.
+2. Staleness check must keep working (IN SCOPE): `src/testproject1/agents/tools/datasource_health_mixin.py:27` flags an index as stale when `last_reindex_triggered_at > update_date`. After the fix: while a manual reindex is running, or if it failed, the index is reported stale/agreement exactly as for scheduled runs; after a successful manual reindex, the index is NOT reported stale (`update_date` set on completion must be later than the trigger stamp). Cover both with tests (see `tests/testproject1/agents/tools/kb/test_search_kb.py`, `tests/testproject1/agents/tools/code/test_code_tools_health.py`).
+3. OUT OF SCOPE: whether failed / guardrail-blocked runs should move "Last indexed". Keep current behaviour.
+4. Verification applies to manual reindex of ANY datasource type (code/git, SVN, Confluence/Jira, Google, Git FAQ, XWiki, SharePoint, Azure DevOps, provider datasources), full and incremental where available.
+5. TP1-10937 (scheduler jobs not running) is a different issue - do not touch scheduler execution logic.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+1. A manual reindex of any datasource type persists `last_reindex_triggered_at`, stamped at trigger time, matching the scheduled-run behaviour.
+2. Refreshing / reopening the datasource returns the new value from the API (persisted, not in-memory).
+3. Stamping does not bump `update_date`; after a successful reindex the datasource is not reported stale by the health mixin; during/after a failed reindex it is.
+4. Scheduled reindex behaviour and existing tests (e.g. `TestReindexActorStampsTriggeredAt` in `tests/testproject1/triggers/actors/test_actor_datasource.py`) are not regressed.
+5. Unit tests cover the manual stamping for the router/service entry points and the staleness interaction.
+
+## Hints
+- Prefer one shared place for the manual stamp (a common helper/service every manual reindex goes through) over copy-pasting into each router, if such a place exists; otherwise follow the actor pattern.
+- Mind the scheduler path: if the shared place is also used by actors, do not stamp twice in a way that changes semantics.
+
+(Source file: `/home/dev7/testproject1-dev/TP1-14857_prompt_backend.md`)
