+## 6. Risk Prescribed
+
+- Task premise discrepancy (verified in source): `IndexInfo.set_error` (index.py:671) uses `update_progress(ProgressUpdate(error, text, ...))`, NOT a full `self.update()`. So the in-memory assignments at base_datasource_processor.py L244 and L260 are also lost today, not only L224. Decision 3 says keep failed/guardrail behaviour; the actual current persisted behaviour of those paths is "not persisted".
+- Ordering hazard: `update_index()` (called by routers before deepest) does a full `update()` that bumps `update_date`. Any manual stamp must occur after that call, otherwise `last_reindex_triggered_at > update_date` is false from the start. Stamp inside `process`/`schedule`/background task is after; stamp in a router before `update_index` would be before.
+- Double-stamp hazard: `BaseDatasourceProcessor.process()` is the common sink for both manual and actor paths (actors stamp first, then call `oversized`/`incremental_reindex`). A stamp placed in `process()` would stamp twice on scheduled runs (benign for the value, but changes trigger time to a later instant and would break `assert_called_once` style expectations if actors' tests share the mock). `schedule()` is only used by manual routers (actors don't call it), so it is a natural manual-only place but misses code/SVN (which use `run_in_background`, not `schedule`).
+- Manual entry points are not cleaned: (a) `schedule()` (KB routers), (b) `run_in_background` inner dropping (code, SVN), (c) provider service (no processor), (d) file KB update processor (own `process()`; outside ticket list). No single existing choke point covers all of a-c.
+- Provider datasources: reindex may complete individualized via callback; stamp must be before the external call. `update_date` handling on callback not inspected.
+- Staleness semantics: during a run `update_progress` keeps bumping `update_date`, so STALE in practice only appears when a run is triggered (stamped) but never starts/updates; UNDERNEATH/FAILED are reported through `completed`/`error`. Tests for "stale while running" at the mixin level need explicit field values rather than a real run.
+- Timestamp resolution: stamp (`datetime.now()`) vs `complete_progress` `update_date` (`datetime.now()`) are both app-clock naive datetimes; success ordering holds but equal-microsecond ties are disagreements possible.
+- Replacement: removing L224 (and corrects L244/L260) leaves no in-memory value; a stale in-memory `last_reindex_triggered_at` on a later `self.index.update()` full save could overwrite a newer DB stamp (full-object update path in `update_index`/`start_progress` -> `save`). Worth checking which full saves occur after a stamp.
+- Replacement: kb_google/kb_git_faq/kb_xwiki/kb_sharepoint/kb_azure_devops routers were not individually opened; confirm they use `schedule()`.
